From 1949de629e9a0de935928a92558ec386b9f34e0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yang Tsao <yang@fydeos.io>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 16:50:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 65/75] am: give ARC containers a longer process-start budget

PROC_START_TIMEOUT is not just a report threshold -- AMS kills the process that
misses it. In the container that turns a slow start into a guaranteed app hang:
an ad SDK calls WebSettings.getDefaultUserAgent() on the main thread, which
blocks until WebView's sandboxed renderer is up; the renderer misses 10 s, AMS
kills it and retries, so the blocked JNI call can never complete and the app
ANRs on the next input event.

Measured on an ARC board: an ordinary process binds in 12-18 ms, but the first
WebView sandboxed renderer after a cold start takes 4509 ms (the second, warm,
takes 3 ms) -- so under memory pressure, when WebView's pages have been evicted
to zram, that cold cost can cross 10 s. AOSP already concedes the environment
can be slow: PROC_START_TIMEOUT_WITH_WRAPPER is 1200 s.

Scoped to IS_CONTAINER so other targets keep stock timing, and left multiplied
by HW_TIMEOUT_MULTIPLIER. Deliberately not raised via ro.hw_timeout_multiplier,
which would also inflate the ANR stack-dump budget and NATIVE_DUMP_TIMEOUT_MS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I5d265905f9d0eef8f591d11bdadbbff953c16075
---
 .../server/am/ActivityManagerService.java     | 22 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityManagerService.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityManagerService.java
index 1dd5a23e4791..06b8509ffb13 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityManagerService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityManagerService.java
@@ -502,7 +502,27 @@ public class ActivityManagerService extends IActivityManager.Stub
 
     // How long we wait for a launched process to attach to the activity manager
     // before we decide it's never going to come up for real.
-    static final int PROC_START_TIMEOUT = 10 * 1000 * Build.HW_TIMEOUT_MULTIPLIER;
+    // fydroid: ARC needs a longer process-start budget than a phone.
+    //
+    // The stock 10 s is not just a report threshold -- AMS KILLS the process that
+    // misses it ("am_kill ... -10000, start timeout"). In the container that turns
+    // a slow start into a guaranteed app hang: an ad SDK calls
+    // WebSettings.getDefaultUserAgent() on the main thread, which blocks until
+    // WebView's sandboxed renderer is up; the renderer misses 10 s, AMS kills it
+    // and retries, so the blocked JNI call can never complete and the app ANRs on
+    // the next input event. Measured on device (Asphalt 8, Crossy Road): repeated
+    // am_process_start_timeout on com.android.webview:sandboxed_process0 at
+    // exactly 10.0 s, each followed by an ANR. gservices has missed the same
+    // budget, so this is not WebView-specific -- process start here is simply
+    // slower than the constant assumes, and killing the victim makes it worse.
+    //
+    // AOSP already concedes the environment can be slow: PROC_START_TIMEOUT_WITH_WRAPPER
+    // below is 1200 s. Scoped to IS_CONTAINER so non-ARC targets keep stock timing,
+    // and left multiplied by HW_TIMEOUT_MULTIPLIER so ro.hw_timeout_multiplier still
+    // scales it. Deliberately NOT raised via that property, which would also inflate
+    // the ANR stack-dump budget and NATIVE_DUMP_TIMEOUT_MS.
+    static final int PROC_START_TIMEOUT =
+            (Build.IS_CONTAINER ? 30 : 10) * 1000 * Build.HW_TIMEOUT_MULTIPLIER;
     // How long we wait to kill an application zygote, after the last process using
     // it has gone away.
     static final int KILL_APP_ZYGOTE_DELAY_MS = 5 * 1000;
